{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017iBwCQz7vFhEMCsJnPPMDF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me try to fetch the OpenReview default form and CoRL statistics page for more specific information. But I think I have enough context now. Let me compile what I know about CoRL review standards:\n\nFrom my knowledge and the search results:\n- CoRL is a competitive robotics conference, typically ~25-35% acceptance rate\n- CoRL uses OpenReview\n- The review form typically has: Overall Rating (1-10 scale similar to other ML conferences), Confidence score, and dimensions like novelty, significance, clarity, soundness\n- The typical scale: 1-3 reject range, 4-5 borderline, 6-7 accept range, 8-10 strong accept range\n\n\n- Based on OpenReview's standard form and CoRL's reviewer guidelines, the 1-10 rating scale maps to acceptance decisions where 8-9 indicates strong accept, 6-7 covers the accept range, 5 is borderline, and 3-4 represents rejection, with the acceptance rate hovering around 25-35%.\n\nThe key evaluation dimensions are Novelty/Significance, Technical Soundness, Clarity, Experimental Evaluation, and Relevance to Robot Learning. Now I'm ready to assign the reviewers—R1 will focus on methods and experiments while R2 handles novelty and related work—then run them through a structured roundtable process starting with independent reviews, followed by cross-sharing, convergence, and finally synthesis.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2200,"cache_read_input_tokens":62834,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2200},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de8902b1-95db-4d6b-992d-38f39dba7b46","uuid":"0bd32809-bc80-4a13-b168-3e25af2743e2"}
