{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ahmm2ULPJzkMvPaGhVu6CT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260525T223036\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: borderline (5-6/10)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-25T23:09:44.446279+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\"\n9\t\n10\t**Venue**: CoRL 2026 (acceptance rate ~36%, OpenReview 1-10 scale)\n11\t**Review mode**: Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n12\t**Dream ID**: 20260525T223036\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n17\t\n18\t### R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n19\t\n20\t**Summary**: The paper presents a large-scale empirical study (70+ experiments, 6000+ episodes) diagnosing systematic contamination of the CAR metric in VLA safety evaluation, proposes dfCAR as a corrective metric, and demonstrates SDF auxiliary training as both a diagnostic probe and partial solution via a two-phase strategy. The diagnostic contribution is strong and well-evidenced; the constructive contribution has meaningful statistical gaps.\n21\t\n22\t**Strengths**:\n23\t- Diagnostic finding is compelling and well-quantified across 3 model families, 2 scenes, and 6000+ episodes\n24\t- LoRA attribution experiment (Table 4) is a clean ablation: LoRA-only CAR ≈ zero-shot baseline (27% vs 26.8%), confirming the full +25pp is from SDF\n25\t- 4-point λ curve (Table 6) provides dose-response evidence with clear diminishing returns pattern\n26\t- Two-phase result (TSR=48%, CAR=45%) occupies a previously unachievable region of the TSR-CAR plane\n27\t- Cross-scene validation (object_pudding + spatial_bowl) shows consistent SDF effect (+25-32pp CAR)\n28\t- Per-task analysis reveals non-obvious EE-only limitation (bbq_sauce -24pp), adding depth\n29\t\n30\t**Weaknesses**:\n31\t- **Phase 2 single seed**: The paper's most important constructive result (TSR=48%, CAR=45%) rests on 1 seed. At minimum 2 additional seeds are needed to establish reliability\n32\t- **OpenVLA asymmetry**: Zero-shot evaluation confounds \"fake safety is architecture-general\" with \"OOD models freeze.\" A frozen OOD model trivially has low displacement — this doesn't prove CAR contamination is architecture-general in the same way as Pi0's SDF-induced collapse. The paper should either fine-tune OpenVLA or explicitly scope the claim\n33\t- **EE-only paradox unresolved**: The paper establishes EE-only SDF has structural limitations (bbq_sauce -24pp, arm-body collisions) then uses EE-only SDF successfully for the two-phase strategy, without reconciling when/why it works vs. fails\n34\t- **dfCAR threshold justification is thin**: δ=0.1m is chosen as \"minimum displacement needed to complete any SafeLIBERO task\" but no sensitivity analysis is presented. What happens at δ=0.05m or δ=0.2m?\n35\t- **No evaluation of published safety methods**: Applying dfCAR to existing methods (AEGIS, SafeVLA) would transform the finding from self-diagnosis to field-wide impact\n36\t- **spatial_bowl single seed**: Cross-scene generalization claim rests on 1 seed for the second scene\n37\t\n38\t**Questions for Authors**:\n39\t1. What is the variance of the Phase 2 result across different Phase 1 checkpoints or seeds?\n40\t2. Does the 0.1m threshold produce qualitatively different conclusions than 0.05m or 0.2m?\n41\t3. How does AEGIS (which reports high CAR with non-trivial TSR) fare under dfCAR?\n42\t\n43\t**Scoring (R1)**:\n44\t- Quality: 6/10\n45\t- Clarity: 7/10\n46\t- Significance: 6/10\n47\t- Relevance: 8/10\n48\t- Overall: 6/10\n49\t- Confidence: 4/5\n50\t\n51\t---\n52\t\n53\t### R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n54\t\n55\t**Summary**: The paper quantifies a systematic bias in CAR (fake safety from action collapse and geometric path safety) across three VLA families, proposes a displacement-filtered metric, and demonstrates SDF auxiliary training as a partial solution. The diagnostic finding is useful but conceptually straightforward once stated; the methodological contributions are incremental combinations of known techniques; and the paper is severely under-cited (5 references).\n56\t\n57\t**Strengths**:\n58\t- Compelling empirical observation with immediate practical consequences for VLA safety evaluation\n59\t- Thorough experimental methodology for diagnostic claims (multiple independent validation signals)\n60\t- Two-phase training result is genuinely interesting — 81% safety retention after removing auxiliary loss is non-trivial\n61\t- Cross-architecture validation with OpenVLA adds breadth\n62\t- Per-task collision transfer analysis reveals non-obvious geometric limitations\n63\t\n64\t**Weaknesses**:\n65\t- **Limited novelty**: The \"fake safety\" observation is conceptually straightforward once stated — a metric that counts non-events is trivially satisfied by inaction. Action collapse is well-documented. The contribution is quantification on a specific benchmark, not discovery of a new phenomenon\n66\t- **dfCAR is a minimal methodological contribution**: Adding a displacement threshold is the obvious fix. No formal connection to measurement theory, construct validity, or threshold selection theory\n67\t- **SDF auxiliary loss is an incremental combination**: SDF-based collision costs are standard in motion planning (CHOMP, DiffCo, RAMP). Applying SDF as auxiliary VLA training loss via LoRA is a straightforward adaptation without new formulations\n68\t- **Two-phase strategy not positioned relative to curriculum learning**: Pre-training with auxiliary objective then removing it is well-known (scheduled auxiliary losses, progressive training). The paper does not cite or discuss this extensive prior work, making the contribution appear more novel than it is\n69\t- **Only 5 references**: Grossly insufficient for any top venue. Missing entire literatures:\n70\t  - Safe RL / constrained RL (CPO, Lagrangian CMDPs, safety critics)\n71\t  - Curriculum learning (Bengio et al. 2009, scheduled auxiliary losses)\n72\t  - SDF in robotics beyond CHOMP (DiffCo, C-space distance fields)\n73\t  - Metric gaming / Goodhart's Law in ML evaluation\n74\t  - Other manipulation benchmarks with safety metrics\n75\t  - Full-body collision detection in learning-based manipulation\n76\t- **Safety-performance tradeoff under-analyzed**: Even the best result (TSR 48%) is a 26pp degradation. No mechanistic analysis of *why* SDF loss degrades task performance so severely\n77\t- **Narrow scope**: Only SafeLIBERO (2 scenes from 1 benchmark). Claims about \"VLA safety evaluation\" are broader than the evidence\n78\t\n79\t**Questions for Authors**:\n80\t1. Does the original SafeLIBERO/AEGIS paper already discuss or account for action collapse when reporting CAR?\n81\t2. What happens with Phase 1 durations other than 10K (e.g., 2K, 5K)? Is there a principled stopping criterion?\n82\t3. At λ=0.1, fake safe rate rises to 8% — is this SDF-induced action collapse? If so, there's a non-monotonic genuine-safety vs λ relationship that deserves analysis\n83\t4. SafeLIBERO has 32 scenarios. Why only 2? Selection bias?\n84\t5. Has oracle SDF refinement been tested on Pi0.5 (which actually moves) rather than only on collapsed Pi0?\n85\t\n86\t**Scoring (R2)**:\n87\t- Originality: 5/10\n88\t- Clarity: 7/10\n89\t- Significance: 6/10\n90\t- Relevance: 8/10\n91\t- Overall: 5/10\n92\t- Confidence: 4/5\n93\t\n94\t---\n95\t\n96\t## Step 2: Cross-Sharing — Key Agreements and Divergences\n97\t\n98\t### Agreements (both reviewers)\n99\t1. **Diagnostic contribution is the paper's primary strength** — the fake safety finding is well-evidenced and practically important\n100\t2. **Phase 2 single seed is a serious concern** — the most important constructive claim lacks statistical backing\n101\t3. **OpenVLA zero-shot is problematic** — confounds OOD failure with metric-general bias\n102\t4. **High relevance to CoRL** (both score 8/10) — VLA safety is timely, the problem directly affects evaluation practices\n103\t5. **Paper is well-written** (both score clarity 7/10) — logical narrative arc, clear experimental methodology\n104\t6. **EE-only limitation is acknowledged but unresolved** — the paper identifies the problem without fully reconciling it with the SDF success claims\n105\t\n106\t### Divergences\n107\t1. **Novelty assessment**: R1 is more neutral (doesn't heavily penalize novelty); R2 argues the observation is \"conceptually straightforward once stated\" and penalizes more strongly (Originality 5/10)\n108\t2. **dfCAR as contribution**: R1 considers it well-motivated; R2 considers it a minimal/obvious fix\n109\t3. **Related work severity**: R1 doesn't heavily flag the 5-reference issue; R2 considers it a critical failure (missing safe RL, curriculum learning, SDF in robotics, metric gaming literatures)\n110\t4. **Overall score**: R1 gives 6/10 (borderline accept); R2 gives 5/10 (marginally below)\n111\t\n112\t---\n113\t\n114\t## Step 3: Convergence — Reconciliation\n115\t\n116\tAfter cross-examining both positions:\n117\t\n118\t1. **On novelty**: R2's critique is well-founded. The core insight (inaction-based metrics are trivially satisfied) is indeed conceptually simple. However, R1 correctly notes that the *quantification* across 3 model families with 6000+ episodes transforms a theoretical concern into actionable evidence. **Consensus**: The novelty is moderate — \"known issue, now rigorously quantified\" rather than \"new discovery.\" This is legitimate but below the bar for a strong accept at a 36% acceptance rate venue.\n119\t\n120\t2. **On related work**: R2's concern is decisive. Five references for a CoRL submission is untenable. The missing connections to safe RL, curriculum learning, and SDF motion planning literature are not cosmetic — they affect how readers assess the contribution's positioning and novelty. **Consensus**: This is a major weakness that must be addressed.\n121\t\n122\t3. **On Phase 2 single seed**: Both reviewers agree. This is the paper's most important constructive claim and it cannot rest on n=1. **Consensus**: Critical weakness.\n123\t\n124\t4. **On OpenVLA**: Both reviewers flag this. A zero-shot OOD model freezing is expected behavior, not evidence of a metric-general bias. **Consensus**: The claim should be scoped as \"fake safety occurs across models\" (empirically true) rather than \"CAR is systematically contaminated across architectures\" (which requires same-distribution evaluation).\n125\t\n126\t5. **On overall score**: Averaging R1 (6) and R2 (5) gives 5.5. Given the severity of the related work gap and the single-seed Phase 2, we converge at **5.5/10 → rounded to 6/10 (marginally above threshold)**, contingent on the diagnostic contribution's practical value outweighing the novelty and completeness concerns.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n131\t\n132\t### Final Scores (CoRL 1-10 scale)\n133\t\n134\t| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n135\t|-----------|----|----|-----------|\n136\t| Originality | — | 5 | **5** |\n137\t| Quality | 6 | — | **6** |\n138\t| Clarity | 7 | 7 | **7** |\n139\t| Significance | 6 | 6 | **6** |\n140\t| Relevance | 8 | 8 | **8** |\n141\t| **Overall** | **6** | **5** | **5-6** |\n142\t| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | **4/5** |\n143\t\n144\t### Overall Recommendation: **Borderline (5-6/10)**\n145\t\n146\tThe paper would be a weak/borderline submission at CoRL 2026 in its current form.\n147\t\n148\t### What Works\n149\t- The fake safety finding is **important and well-quantified**. If the VLA safety community adopts dfCAR, evaluation practices will meaningfully improve. The 98-99.6% fake safety rate across 3 model families is a striking and actionable finding.\n150\t- The experimental scale (70+ experiments, 6000+ episodes) is thorough for the diagnostic claims.\n151\t- The two-phase training result is the most novel element and could anchor a stronger paper if replicated.\n152\t\n153\t### What Needs Work (ranked by priority)\n154\t\n155\t**Critical (must fix)**:\n156\t1. **Related work**: Expand from 5 to 25+ references. Must cover: safe RL/constrained RL, curriculum learning/progressive training, SDF in motion planning (beyond CHOMP), metric gaming/Goodhart's Law in ML, other VLA safety methods, full-body collision detection\n157\t2. **Phase 2 multi-seed**: Replicate across ≥3 seeds. The TSR=48%/CAR=45% claim is load-bearing for the \"partial solution\" framing\n158\t3. **OpenVLA scoping**: Either fine-tune OpenVLA (preferred) or explicitly scope the claim to \"fake safety is observed across architectures\" without implying it's the same phenomenon\n159\t\n160\t**Major (strongly recommended)**:\n161\t4. **dfCAR threshold sensitivity**: Show results at δ ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.2}m to demonstrate robustness\n162\t5. **Apply dfCAR to published methods**: Evaluate AEGIS or SafeVLA under dfCAR — this would dramatically increase impact\n163\t6. **spatial_bowl multi-seed**: At least 2 seeds for cross-scene claim\n164\t7. **Two-phase mechanism analysis**: Why does safety \"stick\" after removing SDF loss? Probing LoRA weight structure (e.g., weight similarity, gradient alignment) would strengthen the contribution\n165\t\n166\t**Minor**:\n167\t8. **Novelty framing**: Position honestly as \"rigorous quantification of a suspected problem + preliminary solution direction\" rather than implying the observation itself is novel\n168\t9. **Curriculum learning connection**: Explicitly discuss relationship to scheduled auxiliary losses and progressive training\n169\t10. **λ=0.1 analysis**: The rising fake safe rate at high λ suggests SDF-induced conservatism approaching collapse — worth analyzing\n170\t\n171\t### Acceptance Probability Estimate\n172\t- **Current form**: 25-35% (below average acceptance probability at a 36% venue)\n173\t- **With critical fixes (1-3)**: 45-55%\n174\t- **With critical + major fixes (1-7)**: 60-70%\n175\t\n176\t### Key Insight for Authors\n177\tThe paper's core tension is that the **diagnostic contribution is strong but not novel enough to carry the paper alone**, while the **constructive contribution (SDF + two-phase) is novel enough but not statistically mature**. The path to acceptance requires either (a) strengthening the diagnostic impact by applying dfCAR to published methods (transforming it from self-diagnosis to field audit), or (b) maturing the constructive contribution with multi-seed replication and mechanistic analysis, or ideally both.\n178\t\n179\t---\n180\t\n181\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n182\t\n183\tComparing paper content against `review_context.yaml` planned experiments:\n184\t\n185\t| Experiment | In Paper? | Quality |\n186\t|-----------|-----------|---------|\n187\t| A1 EE Displacement Re-eval (50ep) | ✅ Table 1 | Good — cross-validates with 800ep obstacle disp |\n188\t| OpenVLA Zero-shot (200ep) | ✅ Tables 2-3 | Adequate but zero-shot limits generalization claim |\n189\t| LoRA Baseline (2 seeds) | ✅ Table 4 | Good — clean attribution control |\n190\t| Phase 2 λ=0 Training | ✅ Table 7 | Training described, loss trajectory discussed |\n191\t| Phase 2 λ=0 Eval (200ep) | ✅ Tables 7-8 | **Single seed — critical gap** |\n192\t| spatial_bowl A1 Eval (200ep) | ✅ Tables 9-10 | **Single seed — weakness** |\n193\t| Lambda=0.1 Training + Eval | ✅ Table 6 | Adequate — completes 4-point curve |\n194\t\n195\tAll planned experiments are covered in the paper. The coverage is complete but the seed counts for Phase 2 and spatial_bowl are insufficient for the claims made.\n196\t\n197\t---\n198\t\n199\t## 引用验证结果\n200\t\n201\t**统计**: 40 篇文献 / 29 处引用 / 在线验证 40/40 篇\n202\t\n203\t### 问题 (24)\n204\t\n205\t- ⚠️ `liu2023libero` — 元数据不匹配: first_author: bib=liu vs api=feng\n206\t- ❌ `karwowski2024goodhart` — 在线验证未找到匹配（疑似虚假引用）: best match similarity 0.29 below threshold 0.82\n207\t- ⚠️ `geoaware2025` — 元数据不匹配: first_author: bib=abouzeid vs api=ali\n208\t- ❌ `stemvla2026` — 在线验证未找到匹配（疑似虚假引用）: no results from any source\n209\t- ❌ `evo02025` — 在线验证未找到匹配（疑似虚假引用）: best match similarity 0.31 below threshold 0.82\n210\t- ⚠️ `vasilopoulos2023ramp` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n211\t- ⚠️ `chen2026cssdfnet` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n212\t- ⚠️ `jacquet2025neuralnmpc` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n213\t- ⚠️ `chen2025samp` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n214\t- ⚠️ `ruan2025profrobot` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n215\t- ⚠️ `depthvla2025` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n216\t- ⚠️ `li2025qdepth` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n217\t- ⚠️ `li2025spatialforcing` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n218\t- ⚠️ `3dcavla2025` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n219\t- ⚠️ `geoaware2025` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n220\t- ⚠️ `spatialvla2025` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n221\t- ⚠️ `gstvla2026` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n222\t- ⚠️ `stemvla2026` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n223\t- ⚠️ `stvla2026` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n224\t- ⚠️ `evo02025` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n225\t- ⚠️ `savla2026` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n226\t- ⚠️ `augvla3d2026` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n227\t- ⚠️ `evodepth2026` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n228\t- ⚠️ `chen2026dfmvla` — 在 .bib 中但正文未引用\n229\t\n230\t### ✅ 通过 (35)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DpquHRciCqyWfEg2rG3pto","session_id":"de8902b1-95db-4d6b-992d-38f39dba7b46","uuid":"5e413643-7a29-4055-b0ee-1aee967d4ed6","timestamp":"2026-05-26T03:20:24.869Z"}
