{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L53K4XARGYJLJhNh76Lo6V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MMkaUS6oJEnxB7Bd14PE3r","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 cross-reads R2 review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1). You previously reviewed the paper \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\" focusing on methods and experiments. Your original rating was 4/10 (Weak Reject).\n\nHere is your original review summary:\n- Core Claim 1 (fake safety) is well-supported with 800 episodes\n- dfCAR is clean and practical with bimodal gap ensuring threshold robustness\n- SDF redundancy claim is underpowered (n=3 seeds, no equivalence testing, Welch t-test p=0.35)\n- Only 2 scenes, 1 benchmark, OpenVLA blocked\n- A1 checkpoint deleted so can't re-verify\n- Catastrophic TSR collapse underanalyzed\n- ~40-50% of planned experiments complete\n- Overall: valid observation but insufficient statistical power for headline negative finding\n\n---\n\nNow here is Reviewer 2's (R2) review. Read it and respond with:\n1. **Points of agreement with R2** (brief)\n2. **Points of disagreement with R2** (brief, explain why)\n3. **New concerns raised by R2 that you find valid**\n4. **R2 concerns you disagree with** (explain why)\n5. **Revised rating** (if any change after reading R2, or confirmation of original)\n\n---\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work):\n\n**Summary**: R2 notes the paper diagnoses CAR contamination by action collapse, proposes dfCAR, and presents a negative finding that SDF is redundant when LoRA on collision-free demos suffices.\n\n**Novelty Assessment**: \n- The observation that a frozen robot gets high CAR is \"conceptually obvious once stated\" — a specific instance of Goodhart's Law / reward hacking by doing nothing\n- dfCAR is \"the simplest possible fix\" — threshold filter on displacement, a practitioner would arrive at this in minutes\n- SDF redundancy is confounded by EE-only formulation — the paper establishes EE-only has structural defects, then tests only EE-only for redundancy claim. Circular reasoning.\n- \"Demonstrations encode avoidance\" is expected from IL theory — if demos avoid collisions, BC learns avoidance\n\n**Strengths per R2**: Quantitative rigor on diagnostic claim, striking 99.6% number, clean LoRA attribution ablation, creative oracle design, threshold robustness, honest negative result reporting\n\n**Weaknesses per R2**:\n- Core observation is conceptually obvious \n- dfCAR is minimal methodological contribution; no alternative metrics explored\n- SDF redundancy confounded by EE-only limitation (circular)\n- Narrow scope vs broad claims (title says \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation\" but evidence is 2 models, 1 benchmark, 2 scenes, sim only)\n- No application of dfCAR to published methods (AEGIS, SafeVLA, etc.)\n- \"Demonstrations encode avoidance\" finding is undertheorized; no engagement with IL literature\n- TSR collapse underanalyzed\n- Single/dual-seed for key claims\n\n**Related Work Gaps identified by R2**:\n- Goodhart's Law / reward hacking / specification gaming literature\n- Conservative/frozen policy failure in offline RL (CQL, BCQ)\n- Safe RL (CPO, Lagrangian CMDP)\n- Full-body collision detection literature\n- IL theory on demonstration content (DAgger, MaxEntIRL, causal confusion)\n- Curriculum learning for Phase 2 ablation design\n- Action collapse / mode collapse in generative policies\n- Other VLA safety benchmarks\n\n**R2's Scores**: Novelty 2/4, Presentation 3/4, Overall 4/10, Confidence 4/5\n\n**R2's one-liner**: \"A well-executed empirical diagnosis of an obvious metric contamination problem, with a minimal fix and a confounded negative finding, that does not clear the novelty bar for a top robotics venue in its current scope.\"\n\n---\n\nNow provide your cross-reading response (items 1-5 above). Keep it focused and under 500 words.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10643,"cache_read_input_tokens":65034,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10643},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de8902b1-95db-4d6b-992d-38f39dba7b46","uuid":"8ac3d494-3274-4104-913c-8d64fb2a7ca3"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aeefde04c740f196e","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMkaUS6oJEnxB7Bd14PE3r","description":"R1 cross-reads R2 review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1). You previously reviewed the paper \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\" focusing on methods and experiments. Your original rating was 4/10 (Weak Reject).\n\nHere is your original review summary:\n- Core Claim 1 (fake safety) is well-supported with 800 episodes\n- dfCAR is clean and practical with bimodal gap ensuring threshold robustness\n- SDF redundancy claim is underpowered (n=3 seeds, no equivalence testing, Welch t-test p=0.35)\n- Only 2 scenes, 1 benchmark, OpenVLA blocked\n- A1 checkpoint deleted so can't re-verify\n- Catastrophic TSR collapse underanalyzed\n- ~40-50% of planned experiments complete\n- Overall: valid observation but insufficient statistical power for headline negative finding\n\n---\n\nNow here is Reviewer 2's (R2) review. Read it and respond with:\n1. **Points of agreement with R2** (brief)\n2. **Points of disagreement with R2** (brief, explain why)\n3. **New concerns raised by R2 that you find valid**\n4. **R2 concerns you disagree with** (explain why)\n5. **Revised rating** (if any change after reading R2, or confirmation of original)\n\n---\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work):\n\n**Summary**: R2 notes the paper diagnoses CAR contamination by action collapse, proposes dfCAR, and presents a negative finding that SDF is redundant when LoRA on collision-free demos suffices.\n\n**Novelty Assessment**: \n- The observation that a frozen robot gets high CAR is \"conceptually obvious once stated\" — a specific instance of Goodhart's Law / reward hacking by doing nothing\n- dfCAR is \"the simplest possible fix\" — threshold filter on displacement, a practitioner would arrive at this in minutes\n- SDF redundancy is confounded by EE-only formulation — the paper establishes EE-only has structural defects, then tests only EE-only for redundancy claim. Circular reasoning.\n- \"Demonstrations encode avoidance\" is expected from IL theory — if demos avoid collisions, BC learns avoidance\n\n**Strengths per R2**: Quantitative rigor on diagnostic claim, striking 99.6% number, clean LoRA attribution ablation, creative oracle design, threshold robustness, honest negative result reporting\n\n**Weaknesses per R2**:\n- Core observation is conceptually obvious \n- dfCAR is minimal methodological contribution; no alternative metrics explored\n- SDF redundancy confounded by EE-only limitation (circular)\n- Narrow scope vs broad claims (title says \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation\" but evidence is 2 models, 1 benchmark, 2 scenes, sim only)\n- No application of dfCAR to published methods (AEGIS, SafeVLA, etc.)\n- \"Demonstrations encode avoidance\" finding is undertheorized; no engagement with IL literature\n- TSR collapse underanalyzed\n- Single/dual-seed for key claims\n\n**Related Work Gaps identified by R2**:\n- Goodhart's Law / reward hacking / specification gaming literature\n- Conservative/frozen policy failure in offline RL (CQL, BCQ)\n- Safe RL (CPO, Lagrangian CMDP)\n- Full-body collision detection literature\n- IL theory on demonstration content (DAgger, MaxEntIRL, causal confusion)\n- Curriculum learning for Phase 2 ablation design\n- Action collapse / mode collapse in generative policies\n- Other VLA safety benchmarks\n\n**R2's Scores**: Novelty 2/4, Presentation 3/4, Overall 4/10, Confidence 4/5\n\n**R2's one-liner**: \"A well-executed empirical diagnosis of an obvious metric contamination problem, with a minimal fix and a confounded negative finding, that does not clear the novelty bar for a top robotics venue in its current scope.\"\n\n---\n\nNow provide your cross-reading response (items 1-5 above). Keep it focused and under 500 words.\n","uuid":"b0ef2165-d3c3-4904-a7ea-274c1a0f88a9","session_id":"de8902b1-95db-4d6b-992d-38f39dba7b46"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1). You previously reviewed the paper \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\" focusing on methods and experiments. Your original rating was 4/10 (Weak Reject).\n\nHere is your original review summary:\n- Core Claim 1 (fake safety) is well-supported with 800 episodes\n- dfCAR is clean and practical with bimodal gap ensuring threshold robustness\n- SDF redundancy claim is underpowered (n=3 seeds, no equivalence testing, Welch t-test p=0.35)\n- Only 2 scenes, 1 benchmark, OpenVLA blocked\n- A1 checkpoint deleted so can't re-verify\n- Catastrophic TSR collapse underanalyzed\n- ~40-50% of planned experiments complete\n- Overall: valid observation but insufficient statistical power for headline negative finding\n\n---\n\nNow here is Reviewer 2's (R2) review. Read it and respond with:\n1. **Points of agreement with R2** (brief)\n2. **Points of disagreement with R2** (brief, explain why)\n3. **New concerns raised by R2 that you find valid**\n4. **R2 concerns you disagree with** (explain why)\n5. **Revised rating** (if any change after reading R2, or confirmation of original)\n\n---\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work):\n\n**Summary**: R2 notes the paper diagnoses CAR contamination by action collapse, proposes dfCAR, and presents a negative finding that SDF is redundant when LoRA on collision-free demos suffices.\n\n**Novelty Assessment**: \n- The observation that a frozen robot gets high CAR is \"conceptually obvious once stated\" — a specific instance of Goodhart's Law / reward hacking by doing nothing\n- dfCAR is \"the simplest possible fix\" — threshold filter on displacement, a practitioner would arrive at this in minutes\n- SDF redundancy is confounded by EE-only formulation — the paper establishes EE-only has structural defects, then tests only EE-only for redundancy claim. Circular reasoning.\n- \"Demonstrations encode avoidance\" is expected from IL theory — if demos avoid collisions, BC learns avoidance\n\n**Strengths per R2**: Quantitative rigor on diagnostic claim, striking 99.6% number, clean LoRA attribution ablation, creative oracle design, threshold robustness, honest negative result reporting\n\n**Weaknesses per R2**:\n- Core observation is conceptually obvious \n- dfCAR is minimal methodological contribution; no alternative metrics explored\n- SDF redundancy confounded by EE-only limitation (circular)\n- Narrow scope vs broad claims (title says \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation\" but evidence is 2 models, 1 benchmark, 2 scenes, sim only)\n- No application of dfCAR to published methods (AEGIS, SafeVLA, etc.)\n- \"Demonstrations encode avoidance\" finding is undertheorized; no engagement with IL literature\n- TSR collapse underanalyzed\n- Single/dual-seed for key claims\n\n**Related Work Gaps identified by R2**:\n- Goodhart's Law / reward hacking / specification gaming literature\n- Conservative/frozen policy failure in offline RL (CQL, BCQ)\n- Safe RL (CPO, Lagrangian CMDP)\n- Full-body collision detection literature\n- IL theory on demonstration content (DAgger, MaxEntIRL, causal confusion)\n- Curriculum learning for Phase 2 ablation design\n- Action collapse / mode collapse in generative policies\n- Other VLA safety benchmarks\n\n**R2's Scores**: Novelty 2/4, Presentation 3/4, Overall 4/10, Confidence 4/5\n\n**R2's one-liner**: \"A well-executed empirical diagnosis of an obvious metric contamination problem, with a minimal fix and a confounded negative finding, that does not clear the novelty bar for a top robotics venue in its current scope.\"\n\n---\n\nNow provide your cross-reading response (items 1-5 above). Keep it focused and under 500 words.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMkaUS6oJEnxB7Bd14PE3r","session_id":"de8902b1-95db-4d6b-992d-38f39dba7b46","uuid":"de709dfb-e266-414f-a673-7205afd8b4ad","timestamp":"2026-05-26T03:22:50.990Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L53K4XARGYJLJhNh76Lo6V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Sm4Xs7YiFws3rsnH9kzSLK","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 cross-reads R1 review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2). You previously reviewed the paper \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\" focusing on novelty and related work. Your original rating was 4/10 (Weak Reject).\n\nHere is your original review summary:\n- Core observation (frozen robot = high CAR) is conceptually obvious, a known instance of Goodhart's Law\n- dfCAR is the simplest possible fix — a threshold filter a practitioner would invent immediately\n- SDF redundancy finding is confounded by EE-only formulation (paper tests only a known-deficient variant)\n- Narrow scope (2 models, 1 benchmark, 2 scenes, sim only) doesn't support general claims\n- Related work severely underdeveloped (missing reward hacking, IL theory, safe RL, metric validity literature)\n- No application of dfCAR to published methods\n- Overall: useful empirical contribution but below novelty bar for top venue\n\n---\n\nNow here is Reviewer 1's (R1) review. Read it and respond with:\n1. **Points of agreement with R1** (brief)\n2. **Points of disagreement with R1** (brief, explain why)\n3. **New concerns raised by R1 that you find valid**\n4. **R1 concerns you disagree with** (explain why)\n5. **Revised rating** (if any change after reading R1, or confirmation of original)\n\n---\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments):\n\n**Summary**: R1 notes the paper identifies CAR contamination, proposes dfCAR, and presents SDF redundancy as a negative finding across 6000+ episodes on SafeLIBERO.\n\n**Strengths per R1**: Well-identified measurement failure (99.6%), dfCAR is clean/practical, negative finding is methodologically sound in structure (4-point lambda curve), oracle experiments well-designed, honest treatment of negative results.\n\n**Weaknesses per R1**:\n1. **Statistical power critically insufficient for SDF redundancy**: n=3 seeds, Welch t-test (Δ=-2.4pp, p=0.35) has extremely low power. Cannot distinguish \"SDF contributes 0pp\" from \"+5pp\" or \"-5pp\". Paper claims absence-of-evidence not evidence-of-absence. Needs TOST equivalence testing.\n2. **Only 2 scenes, 1 benchmark, 1 model family for SDF claim**: spatial_bowl is single-seed (anecdotal).\n3. **OpenVLA missing entirely**: Undermines generalizability claim.\n4. **A1 checkpoint deleted**: Primary Claim 1 evidence can't be reproduced. Internal inconsistency — dfCAR uses EE displacement but A1 analysis uses obstacle displacement.\n5. **Catastrophic TSR collapse (73%→6%) underanalyzed**: Arguably more important finding than SDF redundancy but gets thin analysis.\n6. **200 episodes introduces binomial noise**: ~±7pp per eval, per-seed CIs should be reported.\n7. **Paper has placeholder values**: Multiple tables incomplete.\n8. **Phase 2 only 2 seeds**: Claiming \"zero persistent effect\" from n=2 is statistically irresponsible.\n9. **No comparison of published methods under dfCAR**: Limits practical impact.\n\n**Experiment Coverage Assessment by R1**: ~40-50% of planned experimental program complete with real data. Several core claims depend on imagined/predicted results.\n\n**R1's Scores**: Soundness 2/4, Presentation 3/4, Contribution 2/4, Overall 4/10, Confidence 4/5\n\n**R1's one-liner**: \"A paper with a valid core observation and a practical metric fix, but whose headline negative finding is underpowered and whose experimental program is substantially incomplete.\"\n\n---\n\nNow provide your cross-reading response (items 1-5 above). Keep it focused and under 500 words.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10643,"cache_read_input_tokens":65034,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10643},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de8902b1-95db-4d6b-992d-38f39dba7b46","uuid":"f8168a99-1444-486f-b237-4810b6da3465"}
